# 1️⃣8️⃣ REGULARIZATION ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Now we solve another ML problem:

> What if the model becomes too complicated?

Remember:

```text
Too simple
   ↓
Underfitting

Too complex
   ↓
Overfitting
```

Regularization discourages overly complex parameter values.

The objective becomes approximately:

$$
Loss + Penalty
$$

---

# 1️⃣9️⃣ L2 REGULARIZATION

L2 adds:

$$
\lambda\sum_j w_j^2
$$

to the objective.

Conceptually:

$$
J=
MSE+\lambda\sum_jw_j^2
$$

Large weights become expensive.

Therefore the model is encouraged to keep weights smaller.

---

# 2️⃣0️⃣ L1 REGULARIZATION

L1 adds:

$$
\lambda\sum_j|w_j|
$$

So:

$$
J=
MSE+\lambda\sum_j|w_j|
$$

L1 can drive some weights exactly toward zero.

This can create a sparse model.

---

# 🔥 L1 vs L2

|                         | L1                         | L2                      |   |              |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------- | ----------------------- | - | ------------ |
| Penalty                 | (\sum                      | w                       | ) | \(\sum w^2\) |
| Can produce exact zeros | Often                      | Less commonly           |   |              |
| Useful for              | Sparsity/feature selection | Stable weight shrinkage |   |              |
| Common name             | Lasso                      | Ridge                   |   |              |

---

# 2️⃣1️⃣ REGULARIZATION INTUITION

Imagine two models.

### Model A

```text
w1 = 1
w2 = 2
w3 = 1
```

### Model B

```text
w1 = 100
w2 = 200
w3 = 150
```

Model B has enormous parameters.

Regularization says:

> "Unless those large weights significantly improve prediction, don't use them."

This can improve generalization.

